  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=\u27e8z,\\,y,\\,4x\u27e9$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$",
  "worked_solution": "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=\u27e80,\\,0,\\,1\u27e9.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =\u27e8z,\\,y,\\,4x\u27e9\\cdot \u27e80,\\,0,\\,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84,\\,y,\\,4x\u27e9\\cdot \u27e80,\\,0,\\,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{S}4x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}4x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {[2{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$",
